From e9fc4bf162e17ef8bedfea422f4e1a3f83ec35d4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 12:18:16 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] daemon: read pruned blocks from an archival node when one
 is available

A fork's two chains share every block below the split, so an operator
running a pruned node of one chain and a full node of the other already
holds locally almost everything the pruned node has discarded. Those blocks
were being pulled off the network one at a time through btc-rpc-proxy at
~0.24s each, roughly 950,000 of them on mainnet, while sitting on the same
disk.

`helper_rpc_addr` and `helper_cookie_file` name that node. It is consulted
only for blocks below the prune height, and only ever as a first try: any
failure falls back to the existing path, so the worst case is the behaviour
every install has today. Both options must be set or neither; half a
configuration is dropped with a reason rather than failing per block.

Checked once at startup rather than per block, and rejected with a reason
if it cannot help: a pruned helper holds none of the missing history, and
one whose tip is below this node's prune height has not reached the blocks
being asked for and would look like a boundary at every height.

The upper bound is learned, not configured. The helper follows the other
chain, so it has the shared history and nothing above it, and the split
height is chain-specific and moves with the fork. Asking by hash makes the
boundary discoverable: the first hash the helper reports as unknown fixes a
ceiling and nothing above it is asked again. Only `Block not found` counts;
a timeout or a restart must not fix the ceiling, or a moment's
unavailability would disable the helper for the rest of the build.

Returned bytes are checked against the hash requested. Asking by hash is
already self-limiting, since a block from the other side of the split has a
hash this node has never seen, but nothing else in the pipeline verifies
what a block source returns and this is the one source that is a different
node on a different chain. One header parse removes the question.

Verified on the regtest harness, whose topology is exactly this: archival A
behind pruned B. With the helper configured, peer fetches at the proxy went
from 250 to 0 and all seven Electrum query checks still passed. A helper
that is pruned, unreachable, or half-configured is refused with a reason
and indexing completes unchanged.
